>>What is the weird code displayed in the message above?
> 
> It is called Rot-13, for "rotate 13 places right" after its
> common Net implementation in the minimal 26 letter, two case
> English alphabet.
> 
> It's formal name is a "transposition cypher", by
> the way, but even among them it is simple because the
> transpositions are regular rather than stirred.

I think that is not correct.

In a transposition cipher the characters of the plaintext change
their position, but the characters remain the same.

In a substitution cipher each plaintext character is substituted
by a character from a ciphertext alphabeth (which may or may not
be identical to the plaintext alphabeth).

>>Who invented it?
> 
> It was invented, we are told, by Julius Caeser as a way to
> encrypt his war dispatches, so is also called "Caeser Code"
> [though since the original Latin alphabet wasn't 26
> characters long, and (I think) was monocased, the rotation
> amount was unlikely then to have been precisely 13].

The Caesar Cipher is a substitution cipher where plaintext and
ciphertext alphabeth are the same.  The characters are shifted
by three positions to the right to encode, resp. left to decode.

In rot13 the encoding and decoding functions are the same.

